  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nUpslope rain and snow showers will continue today in the\nmountains and foothills with the lower elevations drying out. \nTemperatures return to near/above normal. While some sunshine is\nlikely on the coastal plain this morning, clouds will move back\nin this afternoon.\n\nLate this afternoon and evening into the overnight another short\nwave trough moves across New England, albeit not very strong. \nAlong and north of the track a relatively narrow band of \nprecipitation is likely to form as forcing for ascent increases.\nTemperatures will be marginal but some light rain and snow is \nlikely. At this time most of the forecast area will experience \nrain but a change to snow is likely for a time in the \nmountains. Amounts will be light. A few snowflakes could \npossibly mix in at lower elevations during the night tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA potent, negatively-tilted shortwave quickly approaches Sunday \nnight into Monday morning. Strong lift will allow precipitation, \nmainly rain, to quickly overspread the region overnight and into \nMonday morning before tapering off south of the mountains in the \nafternoon, although there may still be a few showers after. While \nthe upper-level low will track north of the region, along the \nU.S./Canada border, guidance has been hinting at a secondary surface \nlow developing along the Maine coastline, which may enhance \nprecipitation/winds locally here.\n\nIn addition to the strong lift, forecast soundings depict a small \namount of MUCAPE, so rainfall could be heavy at times. The majority \nof the rain will likely happen in a 4-6 hour window immediately \nahead of the cold front, where a quick 0.5-1.5\" may fall. The \nprogressive nature of the system should put a ceiling on \ntotals, but the Monday morning commute will likely be quite \nmessy given the timing. Upslope flow will likely continue in \nnorthern NH and surrounding mountains during the \nafternoon/evening, which will switch over to light snow as 850 \nmb temperatures drop to -5C by 0Z. Light accumulations in the \nmountains will be possible.\n\nThe other hazard with this next system will be strong winds. Two \ndistinct bursts expected: 1.) from the south immediately ahead of the \ncold front Monday morning, and 2.) westerly in a strong cold air \nadvection regime Monday afternoon-night. Starting with Monday \nmorning, a 45-55 kt S/SSE low-level jet crosses the entire region. \nHowever, recent trends have shown a much stronger jet of around 60-\n80 kts just offshore, so continuing to monitor trends. Downpours may \nhelp momentum transfer some wind to the surface in otherwise rather \ninverted mixing profiles. The more confident period of stronger \nwinds will be from Monday afternoon and Monday evening once the rain \ntapers. Seeing 50-55 kts from the west at 850 mb in a well-mixed \ncold-air advection environment with a good pressure rise couplet, \nplus a potential downslope wind signature on the eastern slopes of \nthe mountains. Combined with a decent signal on the EFI (Extreme \nForecast Index) for strong winds, confidence is increasing in one or \ntwo bursts of strong winds Monday, which may cause isolated tree \ndamage and power outages.\n\nDecreasing winds on Tuesday will signal a quieter period of weather \nwith cooler, but near seasonable temperatures. Low chances for \nprecipitation return on Thursday-Friday, with timing/confidence \nuncertain currently.",
